Below is a ready‑to‑paste JavaScript snippet that shows how you can create a new `ChartSeries` using the `ChartSeriesGenerator` helper and immediately attach it to your chart layout.  
Just replace the placeholder strings (`'["label1","label2"]'`, `'[10,20,30]'`, `'["#ff0000","#00ff00","#0000ff"]'`, and `'{"title":"My Chart"}'`) with the actual values you want to use.

```js
// 1️⃣  Prepare the data you want to feed into the generator.
//     These are *string representations* of JavaScript arrays / objects,
//     exactly what the helper expects.

const labelsString   = '["label1","label2","label3"]';          // <-- your axis labels
const dataString     = '[10, 20, 30]';                         // <-- your data points
const colorString    = '#3498db';                              // <-- your default color
const chartLayoutStr = '{"title":"Sample Chart","width":600}'; // <-- your chart layout

// 2️⃣  Call the helper.  The function returns the newly created series
//     (you can store it, inspect it, or simply ignore the return value
//     if you only care about the side‑effect of adding it to the layout).

const newSeries = ChartSeriesGenerator({
  labels:   labelsString,
  data:     dataString,
  color:    colorString,          // optional – omit if you don't want a custom color
  chartLayout: chartLayoutStr
});

// 3️⃣  If you need to do anything else with the series (e.g., log it),
//     you can now do so.  Otherwise, the series has already been attached
//     to the layout specified above.

console.log('New series created and attached:', newSeries);
```

### How it works

| Step | What happens | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **1️⃣** | You supply *stringified* arrays/objects. | The helper expects plain strings that represent JavaScript literals. |
| **2️⃣** | `ChartSeriesGenerator` parses those strings, builds a `ChartSeries` instance, and attaches it to the layout. | This is the core operation you asked for. |
| **3️⃣** | You can optionally keep a reference to the returned series. | Useful if you want to modify it later or inspect its properties. |

> **Tip:** If you’re working in a Node.js environment or a browser console, you can use `JSON.stringify()` to turn real arrays/objects into the required string format automatically:

```js
const labelsString = JSON.stringify(['label1','label2','label3']);
const dataString   = JSON.stringify([10,20,30]);
const chartLayoutStr = JSON.stringify({ title: 'My Chart', width: 800 });
```

That’s all you need to get a fresh `ChartSeries` up and running in your chart layout!
